/false/crop/3200x1800+0+95/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F3e%2F09%2F0e863e77427ea1f57eca3fb36a51%2Fgettyimages-2297678210.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3200x1800+0+95/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F3e%2F09%2F0e863e77427ea1f57eca3fb36a51%2Fgettyimages-2297678210.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3200x1800+0+95/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F3e%2F09%2F0e863e77427ea1f57eca3fb36a51%2Fgettyimages-2297678210.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3200x1800+0+95/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F3e%2F09%2F0e863e77427ea1f57eca3fb36a51%2Fgettyimages-2297678210.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3200x1800+0+95/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F3e%2F09%2F0e863e77427ea1f57eca3fb36a51%2Fgettyimages-2297678210.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3200x1800+0+95/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F3e%2F09%2F0e863e77427ea1f57eca3fb36a51%2Fgettyimages-2297678210.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3200x1800+0+95/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F3e%2F09%2F0e863e77427ea1f57eca3fb36a51%2Fgettyimages-2297678210.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3200x1800+0+95/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F3e%2F09%2F0e863e77427ea1f57eca3fb36a51%2Fgettyimages-2297678210.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3200x1800+0+95/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F3e%2F09%2F0e863e77427ea1f57eca3fb36a51%2Fgettyimages-2297678210.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3200x1800+0+95/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F3e%2F09%2F0e863e77427ea1f57eca3fb36a51%2Fgettyimages-2297678210.jpg" class="img" alt="(L-R) Nia Otchere-Sarfo, Jordan Rice, Lucia Aremu, Denee Benton, Heather Alicia Simms, Erin Morton, Jasmine Amy Rogers and Patina Miller during the opening night curtain call for Manhattan Theatre Club&amp;#039;s production of &amp;quot;School Girls; Or The African Mean Girls Play&amp;quot; on Broadway at The Samuel J. Friedman Theatre on September 28, 2026 in New York City." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 (L-R) Nia Otchere-Sarfo, Jordan Rice, Lucia Aremu, Denee Benton, Heather Alicia Simms, Erin Morton, Jasmine Amy Rogers and Patina Miller during the opening night curtain call for Manhattan Theatre Club's production of "School Girls; Or The African Mean Girls Play" on Broadway at The Samuel J. Friedman Theatre on September 28, 2026 in New York City.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Bruce Glikas/Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Check out what &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/sections/culture/" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;NPR&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;strong&gt; is watching, reading and listening to this weekend:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;🍿 Movies:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/08/nx-s1-5987771/gentle-monster-film-lea-seydoux" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Gentle Monster&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; follows a loving couple who move to a farmhouse and whose peace shatters when the husband is accused of a crime. Léa Seydoux, who plays the wife named Lucy, discusses her first "complex" lead role in English with &lt;em&gt;Morning Edition&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;📺 TV: &lt;/strong&gt;The new show &lt;a href="http://npr.org/2026/11/12/nx-s1-6036752/cupertino-is-a-stylish-legal-drama-full-of-tech-twists" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Cupertino&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; follows two Silicon Valley lawyers whose new firm represents the underdog, takes on big tech and grapples with the complexities of AI.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;📚 Books:&lt;/strong&gt; Journalist Mona Chalabi spent five years following 10 Americans of different economic standings to uncover what it takes to feel financially secure. Her book, &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/10/nx-s1-6032767/mona-chalabi-ten-lives" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Ten Lives: How What We Have Shapes Who We Are&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, delves into the elusive nature of financial security.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;🎵 Music:&lt;/strong&gt; Rappers Lil Baby, Jack Harlow and SOB x RBE offer three ways to relight a flickering star and chart a path toward creative restoration &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/12/nx-s1-6032893/lil-baby-jack-harlow-sob-rbe-review" target="_blank"&gt;through their respective albums&lt;/a&gt;, released today.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;🎭 Theater:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/10/g-s1-152641/get-in-loser-broadways-newest-mean-girls-are-ready-to-insult-and-enlighten-you" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;School Girls; Or, The African Mean Girls Play&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; wraps up its Broadway run this week. To celebrate, it will have paid livestreams on &lt;a href="https://lolst.org/shows/school-girls" target="_blank"&gt;The League of Live Stream Theater's website&lt;/a&gt;. Set in a 1986 Ghanaian boarding school, the play humorously tackles themes of girlhood and beauty standards.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;❓Quiz:&lt;/strong&gt; Did you actually pay attention this week, or did you just scroll past the headlines? &lt;a href="http://npr.org/2026/11/13/g-s1-153174/tesla-nvidia-huang-arch-nasa-gas-trump-news-quiz" target="_blank"&gt;Prove it&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;3 things to know before you go&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-143201" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 8192;         --source-height: 4608;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+350/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8d%2F34%2F799f46ee4309a19bc6b1f9d0c1da%2Fgettyimages-2270294552.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+350/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8d%2F34%2F799f46ee4309a19bc6b1f9d0c1da%2Fgettyimages-2270294552.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+350/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8d%2F34%2F799f46ee4309a19bc6b1f9d0c1da%2Fgettyimages-227029