99f46ee4309a19bc6b1f9d0c1da%2Fgettyimages-2270294552.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+350/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8d%2F34%2F799f46ee4309a19bc6b1f9d0c1da%2Fgettyimages-2270294552.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+350/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8d%2F34%2F799f46ee4309a19bc6b1f9d0c1da%2Fgettyimages-2270294552.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+350/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8d%2F34%2F799f46ee4309a19bc6b1f9d0c1da%2Fgettyimages-2270294552.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+350/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8d%2F34%2F799f46ee4309a19bc6b1f9d0c1da%2Fgettyimages-2270294552.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+350/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8d%2F34%2F799f46ee4309a19bc6b1f9d0c1da%2Fgettyimages-2270294552.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+350/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8d%2F34%2F799f46ee4309a19bc6b1f9d0c1da%2Fgettyimages-2270294552.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+350/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8d%2F34%2F799f46ee4309a19bc6b1f9d0c1da%2Fgettyimages-2270294552.jpg" class="img" alt="A &amp;quot;For Sale&amp;quot; sign is shown posted outside a property in Pasadena, Calif., on April 7, 2026." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A "For Sale" sign is shown posted outside a property in Pasadena, Calif., on April 7, 2026.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Mario Tama/Getty Images North America                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ol class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;Existing home sales &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/12/nx-s1-6038940/punch-in-the-gut-homes-sales-fall-to-their-lowest-in-16-years" target="_blank"&gt;fell to 3.75 million&lt;/a&gt; in October, marking an 8.8% drop from last year — the lowest sales in 16 years, according to the National Association of Realtors.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;For more than 60 years, researchers at the University of Virginia have &lt;a href="https://www.wbur.org/hereandnow/2026/11/11/child-reincarnation?utm_source=npr.org&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_campaign=national_highlight&amp;utm_content=homepage" target="_blank"&gt;identified children with past-life memories&lt;/a&gt;. A new study suggests that those who died violently or unexpectedly are reincarnated more quickly than others. (via &lt;a href="https://www.wbur.org/" target="_blank"&gt;WBUR&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The American Cancer Society has &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/12/nx-s1-6037709/american-cancer-society-updates-prostate-cancer-screening-guidelines" target="_blank"&gt;recommended routine prostate cancer screenings&lt;/a&gt; for the first time. The group says that blood tests, especially for high-risk groups like Black men and those with a family history, could save lives.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This newsletter was edited by &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/859721331/suzanne-nuyen"target="_blank"   &gt;&lt;em&gt;Suzanne Nuyen&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
