      <title>Control of Congress is at stake today. And, judge to consider motion in Clancy case</title>
      <author>Brittney Melton</author>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/11/03/g-s1-151445/up-first-newsletter-midterms-election-day-iran-war-lindsay-clancy</link>
      <comments>https://text.npr.org/2026/11/03/g-s1-151445/up-first-newsletter-midterms-election-day-iran-war-lindsay-clancy</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.npr.org/2026/11/03/g-s1-151445/up-first-newsletter-midterms-election-day-iran-war-lindsay-clancy</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 11:43:49 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div id="storytext" class="storytext storylocation linkLocation"&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Good morning. You're reading the Up First newsletter. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/newsletter/news" target="_blank"&gt;Subscribe&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; here to get it delivered to your inbox, and &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510318/up-first/" target="_blank"&gt;listen&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; to the Up First podcast for all the news you need to start your day.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Today's top stories&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Election Day has arrived. &lt;/strong&gt;This campaign has the biggest price tag in U.S. history. A record number of lawmakers are stepping down, setting the stage for Democrats to potentially regain control of Congress. All the while, President Trump's popularity is at an all-time low. Here's &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/03/nx-s1-5992757/election-day-live-updates-key-races" target="_blank"&gt;everything you need to know&lt;/a&gt; about today's election, and &lt;a href="http://npr.org/g-s1-151305" target="_blank"&gt;four types of House races to watch&lt;/a&gt; as results come in. NPR has your guide for how to &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/01/g-s1-149978/midterms-election-night" target="_blank"&gt;watch the results&lt;/a&gt; and when polls close, hour by hour.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-151230" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 6804;         --source-height: 3827;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6804x3827+0+354/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fae%2F55%2Fec98ce1e44558904733dc6c91531%2Fgettyimages-2302583836.jpg" class="img" alt="In an aerial view, voters wait in line on the last day of early in-person voting at the Franklin County Board of Elections on November 01, 2026 in Columbus, Ohio. " loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 In an aerial view, voters wait in line on the last day of early in-person voting at the Franklin County Board of Elections on November 01, 2026 in Columbus, Ohio.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Jon Cherry/Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;The president's unpopular policies have rocked the economy&lt;/strong&gt; and made unexpected states like Arkansas, Kansas, Mississippi and Iowa crucial for deciding control of Congress, NPR's Stephen Fowler tells &lt;em&gt;Up First&lt;/em&gt;. Voters are concerned about the war in Iran, rising gas prices and issues like corruption. They expressed feeling neglected by Trump's focus on grand events instead of their priorities. Both parties are &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6027249:x" target="_blank"&gt;investing heavily in numerous House races&lt;/a&gt;. Republicans' closing message asked for two more years to fix issues, and Democrats argued they have already had too much time.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧&lt;strong&gt; Trump said yesterday that if a portion of his supporters voted&lt;/strong&gt;, they could win in a "landslide," but he appeared to recognize the challenges ahead, NPR's Franco Ordoñez says. He spoke in the past tense, which is unlike his usual bombast. Results could take days, and Trump's team will closely monitor Republican turnout in crucial Senate races in places like Ohio, Kansas and South Carolina, where polls will close sooner. Strong turnout &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6027249:x" target="_blank"&gt;might ease Trump's worries&lt;/a&gt;, but close races in South Carolina or Florida could signal a tough night for Republicans, Ordoñez says.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;Race calls are crucial on election night.&lt;/strong&gt; Here's &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/03/g-s1-151311/how-npr-calls-races-2026" target="_blank"&gt;how NPR collaborates with the Associated Press&lt;/a&gt; to make them.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;Just before Election Day, NPR conducted one final check-in &lt;/strong&gt;with swing voters for its &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/series/g-s1-124876/swing-shift" target="_blank"&gt;Swing Shift project&lt;/a&gt;. All of them have cast a vote for Trump in the past decade. They're now rating his presidency while &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/03/nx-s1-6014577/swing-voters-midterm-elections-voting-trump" target="_blank"&gt;considering their choice&lt;/a&gt; when he isn't on the ballot.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;The U.S. Supreme Court rejected the Department of Justice's last-minute request &lt;/strong&gt;to intervene in its &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/02/nx-s1-6023802/supreme-court-california-voting-ballots-trump" target="_blank"&gt;unprecedented feud with California&lt;/a&gt; over voter rolls. The DOJ sought to halt California from processing ballots for thousands of individuals identified as "likely" noncitizens.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;The NPR App has all of NPR's election coverage in one place&lt;/strong&gt; — from the biggest stories across the country to the issues that matter close to home. Browse headlines, playlists, podcast episodes, videos and more, all on the &lt;a href="https://npr.app/stqe/election-2026" target="_blank"&gt;2026 Election tab&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Iran has been leveraging the U.S. midterms in its pressure campaign &lt;/strong&gt;to push Trump back to negotiations. The elections could signal a possible return to conflict with the U.S. and Israel. The Associated Press reports that the U.S. conducted defensive strikes on Iranian targets in the Strait of Hormuz last night, including Qeshm Island, which is a key launch point for Iran's strikes. While NPR could not independently confirm the details, Iran reported explosions on the island. This pattern of strikes has occurred several times in recent months, coinciding with heightened Iranian attacks on shipping vessels in the Strait.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧&lt;strong&gt; Iranian officials say they prefer to return to negotiations.&lt;/strong&gt; The country has offered to reopen the Strait of Hormuz in two days ahead of the seven it proposed in September, if the U.S. re-engages in talks, NPR's Hadeel Al-Shalchi says. The Iranian inflation rate is around 90%, and residents are &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6027249:x" target="_blank"&gt;facing dire economic conditions&lt;/a&gt; alongside fears of potential bombings. As winter approaches, officials are urging millions of Iranians to conserve energy to avoid soaring natural gas prices. They say the country's natural gas networks are unstable due to damage from the war. Al-Shalchi says Tehran is hoping it can wait out the U.S. election and possibly see Trump's party lose control of Congress.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A Massachusetts judge is considering a motion to dismiss the case against Lindsay Clancy&lt;/strong&gt;. Plymouth Superior Court Judge William Sullivan will announce his decision later this month. In court yesterday, Clancy's attorney &lt;a href="https://www.wbur.org/news/2026/10/30/clancy-status-hearing-prosecutors-case" target="_blank"&gt;requested that the judge dismiss the case&lt;/a&gt; or initiate a new trial. Clancy faces first-degree murder charges for the 2023 deaths of her three young children in their Duxbury home. The trial ended in a mistrial in September when jurors failed to reach a unanimous verdict. The Plymouth County district attorney's office has not disclosed its next steps, including whether to seek a new trial or pursue lesser charges. In a recent motion, prosecutors stated they aim to "pursue justice" for Clancy's children.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Picture show&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-150688" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 8000;         --source-height: 4500;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8000x4500+0+414/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F60%2Fdd%2F7ca94032447789d52228608bf99e%2Fnc-shipbreaking-on-the-mudflats-bay-of-bengal-george-steinmetz.jpg" class="img" alt="An old LNG carrier in the final stage of disassembly for scrap metal on the mudflats of the Bay of Bengal, just north of Chattogram, Bangladesh. Breaking a ship like this will take about a year, with a crew of 60 men using blow torches and winches to drag the larger pieces ashore for cutting into smaller pieces. The nearby BSRM smelter melts down the scrap metal from the old boats and produces rebar and rolled steel, while the more valuable non-ferrous metals are sold overseas. The brass from this ship was sold to Hyundai, the ship’s manufacturer in Korea, for use in new ships. From this LNG ship, they will collect a total of 30k tons of metal, plus 2.4k tons of aluminum and 200-300 tons of brass, copper, and stainless steel. Bangladesh has no iron mines and relies on shipbreaking for much of its iron and steel. Shipbreaking requires low wages and high demand for scrap steel. LNG boats are more valuable as they have large quantities of non-ferrous metals that fetch higher prices. Bangladesh had 160 shipbreaking yards before the June 2025 regulation requiring that all new ship purchases after that date be broken using “green methods” requiring international certifications. This forced most of the shipbreakers out of business, and now there are only 10 active shipbreaking yards. The green regulations require a US$4-5 million investment in a thick concrete slab for breaking work, a crane, and toxic liquid storage tanks, as well as higher wages and international certifications. resulting in higher operating costs. This LNG ship was purchased before the June 2025 green deadline. The world’s biggest ship-breaking areas are in Alang, India; #2 is Bangladesh; #3 is Pakistan; and they are all now subject to the same standards. This old-style ship-breaking operation is being done in a rented yard and run by Marine Safety System, but its owner, Mr. Kamal Ahamad, doesn’t want his name or the company’s name mentioned in captions. Kamal works in Bangla" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 An old LNG carrier in the final stage of disassembly for scrap metal on the mudflats of the Bay of Bengal, just north of Chattogram, Bangladesh.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          George Steinmetz/©2026 George Steinmetz                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;The Siena Drone Photo Awards showcase aerial views of our planet each year, highlighting landscapes and human stories often hidden from view. Photographer George Steinmetz won the top prize of 2026 for his photo titled "Shipbreaking on the mudflats, Bay of Bengal," which captures a stranded vessel off the coast of Bangladesh. The image depicts workers around a partially dismantled tanker designed for transporting liquefied natural gas. The photo illustrates the country's shipbreaking industry, where enormous vessels from around the globe are dismantled, and their materials are repurposed. See Steinmetz's work and some of &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/31/g-s1-150698/drone-photo-award-winners" target="_blank"&gt;this year's winning and highly commended photographs&lt;/a&gt; from countries in the Global South.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Behind the story&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;by&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/842041232/jonaki-mehta" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt; Jonaki Mehta&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, education correspondent focused on disabilities and learning differences&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-149096" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 3201;         --source-height: 1801;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3201x1801+0+114/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2Fa0%2F4dab85e941dd800bb21716c15331%2Ffull-grid.jpg" class="img" alt="Top row (from left): Danalyn Garcia of Chicago Public Schools, Lucie Lane of Shawnee Mission Public Schools in Kansas, Jeff Carroll of Newport News City Public Schools in Virginia and Kaori Anderson-Walker of TBD. Bottom row (from left): Paul Stone of Mount Diablo Unified in California, Diana Swanson of Vail School District in Arizona, Kathleen Tejeda of Alvord Unified School District in California and Emily Ellis of Lexington School District Two in South Carolina." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 &lt;strong&gt;Top row (from left):&lt;/strong&gt; Danalyn Garcia of Chicago Public Schools, Lucie Lane of Shawnee Mission Public Schools in Kansas, Jeff Carroll of Newport News City Public Schools in Virginia and Kaori Anderson-Walker of TBD. &lt;strong&gt;Bottom row (from left):&lt;/strong&gt; Paul Stone of Mount Diablo Unified in California, Diana Swanson of Vail School District in Arizona, Kathleen Tejeda of Alvord Unified School District in California and Emily Ellis of Lexington School District Two in South Carolina.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          LA Johnson/NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;For half a century, America hasn't had enough special education teachers. It's a shortage that's only gotten worse as the number of students with disabilities grows rapidly — 8.2 million according to the most recent federal count, or about 1 in 6 students.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Research explains some of why these roles are so hard to fill: These teachers enjoy working with students but spend more time on legally required paperwork and tasks other than teaching. They often don't get paid more despite having more responsibilities than many other teachers. And because there's such a widespread shortage, the teachers who stay in the profession are often handed a bigger load.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;NPR wanted to &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/26/nx-s1-6000653/special-education-teacher-shortage" target="_blank"&gt;go beyond the research&lt;/a&gt; and hear directly from educators, so NPR senior producer Lauren Migaki and I have been exchanging voice memos with more than a dozen teachers from big and small districts around the country. So far, they've shared tales of joy when a student found success, tearful moments when they felt like they had failed, and waking up before dawn to make sure they were prepared.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;For example, Jeffrey Carroll, who teaches in Newport News, Va., said last year, he was teaching students across three grade levels and multiple subjects: second, fourth and fifth. It meant he was constantly running from one classroom to the next. "I just didn't feel like I was teaching, I was never in a class long enough to be a meaningful part of it." This year, he was relieved to only be teaching across two grade levels. "It's been good to support my students in the classroom. I get to do some co-teaching. The lesson plans come to life."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Lauren and I will stay in touch with these teachers throughout the rest of the school year and visit some in their classrooms to get a &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/26/nx-s1-6000653/special-education-teacher-shortage" target="_blank"&gt;better understanding of what it takes&lt;/a&gt; to do this essential job.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;3 things to know before you go&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-150376" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 5616;         --source-height: 3159;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F93%2F2cc2e4644cb7b064d3328a825f6e%2F2026-09-26-nso-gala-079-jpg-fix.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F93%2F2cc2e4644cb7b064d3328a825f6e%2F2026-09-26-nso-gala-079-jpg-fix.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F93%2F2cc2e4644cb7b064d3328a825f6e%2F2026-09-26-nso-gala-079-jpg-fix.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F93%2F2cc2e4644cb7b064d3328a825f6e%2F2026-09-26-nso-gala-079-jpg-fix.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F93%2F2cc2e4644cb7b064d3328a825f6e%2F2026-09-26-nso-gala-079-jpg-fix.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F93%2F2cc2e4644cb7b064d3328a825f6e%2F2026-09-26-nso-gala-079-jpg-fix.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F93%2F2cc2e4644cb7b064d3328a825f6e%